const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Customer segmentation","serviceType":"Data service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/customer-segmentation/","description":"Segmentation for healthcare, pharma, medical devices and publishing. Tailored insights for targeted research, marketing and publication strategy.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Why segment at all?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because undifferentiated outreach is expensive and the groups that matter are usually not the ones an organisation has been assuming. Segmentation identifies where effort compounds and where it is wasted.\n\nThe practical test is whether you can act differently towards different groups. If you cannot, segmentation produces an interesting analysis and no change — which is why we ask what you can act on before we model anything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What kinds of segmentation do you do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Patient segmentation, physician and prescriber segmentation, institutional and market segmentation for biotech, pharmaceutical, hospital and medical device markets, and author, reader and reviewer segmentation for publishers.\n\nMethods include k-means and hierarchical clustering, model-based clustering, latent class analysis, CHAID and decision-tree segmentation, and psychographic profiling alongside the quantitative work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you decide how many segments there are?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By stability rather than by a single fit statistic. Candidate structures are tested across resamples, and the number that reproduces is the number you have.\n\nIt is common for a five-segment model to contain four real segments and one that does not come back. Delivering that fifth one is how organisations end up with a persona and a budget line for a group that was never there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is CHAID, and when would you use it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Chi-squared automatic interaction detection is a decision-tree method that splits a population on the variables that most separate an outcome — producing segments defined by rules rather than by distance to a cluster centre.\n\nIt is the right choice when somebody who is not a data scientist has to assign new records to segments. A rule can be applied in a CRM; a centroid cannot."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How often should segmentation be updated?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Behaviour and markets move, so we recommend reviewing quarterly and rebuilding when the review shows drift, or whenever your product or audience changes materially.\n\nA segmentation nobody revisits becomes an accurate description of a year that has already passed, and it keeps being used because it is there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the segments be used in our existing systems?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Segment assignments can be delivered as fields for a CRM, marketing automation or analytics platform, and where assignable rules are needed the model is built to produce them.\n\nSay so at scoping. Building a model that can be applied in production is a slightly different job from building one that explains a dataset."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/customer-segmentation/"}]}];

